+Concurrent updates are serialized by Postgres FOR UPDATE row locks: concurrent callers block until the lock holder commits, then retry against fresh data — no lost updates. An optional expected_revision enforces optimistic concurrency on top: obtain the opaque revision token from memory_get → memory.revision (or from a prior memory_update result's revision field). Pass it to ensure the update only applies if the row hasn't changed since you read it; if omitted, the update applies unconditionally (last-writer-wins under the row lock). The output revision field is an opaque token — store it and pass it back as expected_revision on the next update to detect concurrent modification. If the target was superseded by a newer version, or if expected_revision is stale, the call is refused with error -32000, data.code="update_conflict". The data carries current_revision (the live version's revision) for stale tokens, and current_id (+ current_revision) when the target was superseded (e.g. by a versioned content update or a note→decision promotion). On refusal nothing is written — re-read the current version, merge your changes, then retry.
